Re-offer the installed tunnel instead of relaying a 401 with no offer.

ipsec_create() is what puts the Security-Server header — the P-CSCF's SPIs and
protected ports — on the 401, and every one of its failure paths returns before
add_security_server_header(). The challenge is relayed either way: it is a final
reply, so tm forwards it whatever the reply route decides, and drop() in an
onreply_route only works on provisional ones. What reaches the UE is a 401 with
no offer.

A UE reads that as a P-CSCF that does not do IPsec. It answers with an
unprotected REGISTER, and the contact that replaces the protected one no longer
satisfies pcscf_is_registered(), so every originating request from it is
answered "403 Forbidden - You must register first with a S-CSCF" while its
terminating leg leaves in the clear. The registration is not recoverable short
of the UE starting over.

The case this was written for is a second 401 for a challenge the UE has
already answered, where building the tunnel again failed. Upstream's two-slot
ipsec_create() now retires the earlier offer and makes a new one there, but
any of its other failure paths (an offer that cannot be removed, SPIs or ports
exhausted, a kernel error) still relays a 401 with no offer. Whatever produced
it, when the contact already carries an installed tunnel, the only thing the
reply had to do was name it. So name it: re-offer the offered tunnel, or the
registered one when the offer is deferred and so not installed, and return
success. A first registration has no complete tunnel in either slot
(contact_tunnel()) and still fails loudly.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
index 1636fe4..f33653f 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
@@ -1320,6 +1320,36 @@ int ipsec_create(struct sip_msg *m, udomain_t *d, int _cflags)
 	ret = IPSEC_CMD_SUCCESS; // all good, set ret to SUCCESS, and exit
 
 cleanup:
+	// Never leave a 401 without an offer the UE can answer over ESP. Every
+	// failure above returns before add_security_server_header(), and a UE that
+	// reads a challenge carrying no Security-Server concludes this P-CSCF does
+	// not do IPsec: it re-registers unprotected, and the contact that replaces
+	// the protected one takes its tunnels with it at expiry. The SIP layer
+	// cannot suppress such a 401: tm relays a final reply whatever the reply
+	// route says.
+	//
+	// When the contact already carries an installed tunnel, name it: re-offer
+	// it and report success, rather than downgrade the UE to no security at
+	// all. The latest one installed is the one to name: the offered tunnel,
+	// unless it is deferred and so not installed (see tunnel_deferred()), else
+	// the registered one.
+	if(ret != IPSEC_CMD_SUCCESS && pcontact != NULL) {
+		ipsec_t *cur_est = contact_tunnel(pcontact->security);
+		ipsec_t *cur = contact_tunnel(pcontact->security_temp);
+
+		if(!cur || tunnel_deferred(cur_est, cur)) {
+			cur = cur_est;
+		}
+		if(cur && add_supported_secagree_header(m) == 0
+				&& add_security_server_header(m, cur) == 0) {
+			LM_WARN("Could not build a tunnel for this 401 on contact [%.*s]; "
+					"re-offering the one already installed (client port %d, "
+					"server port %d, spi_pc %u, spi_ps %u)\n",
+					ci.aor.len, ci.aor.s, cur->port_pc, cur->port_ps,
+					cur->spi_pc, cur->spi_ps);
+			ret = IPSEC_CMD_SUCCESS;
+		}
+	}
 	if(req_sec_params && !sec_stored) {
 		free_security_t(req_sec_params);
 	}
